critically from the issue of multiplicity; a policy to “torture the data until they talk” is a sure-fire way to produce false discoveries (and statisti
cal trauma) (Head et al., 2015). How can study teams and collaborative statisticians avoid this cycle of seemingly endless analyses? More gen
erally, what strategies can academic collaboration units that house these statisticians do to optimize both productivity and joie de vivre in
interdisciplinary relationships?
Several authors have explored what specific collaboration skills in statisticians are crucial for ensuring that partnerships are effective
(e.g., Pomann et al., 2020; Slade et al., 2023), and many have also defined overarching competencies for effective biomedical team science
(e.g., Brasier et al., 2023). In team science settings, statisticians must communicate effectively with nonstatisticians who are experts in their own
domain. We henceforth refer to the latter group as domain experts, as recommended in Vance and Smith (2019). Effective statistical communica
tion requires statisticians and domain experts alike to overcome the curse of knowledge (Shatz, 2023) to create shared understanding (Vance
et al., 2022). For these reasons, training in effective communication strategies (e.g., Derr, 1999; Sharp et al., 2021) is often, though not always, a
formal part of statistical education.
This paper provides guidance for statisticians within academic collaboration units to identify and encourage positive qualities in non
statistician collaborators and to respectfully discourage negative ones. This paper is organized as follows. First, we describe how information was
collected from a large group of collaborative statisticians and used to identify positive and negative qualities and actions of nonstatistician collabo
rators. Second, we describe how and why we translated these qualities into a survey that we then disseminated to a wider group of practicing
statisticians. Third, we present the extent to which these identified qualities are agreed upon by a larger subset of collaborative statisticians. We
conclude with a discussion of the implications and future work.
2 | METHODS
2.1 | Focus groups
At the 2022 annual meeting for the Association of Clinical and Translational Statisticians (ACTStat), two groups of academic collaborative statisti
cians discussed the positive and negative qualities of their collaborations. These focus groups consisted of approximately one to two dozens of
statisticians with highly varied levels of experience. ACTStat is a group of collaborative statisticians (primarily in academia) whose collective mis
sion is to positively impact the health of individuals and the public by collaborating in the design, implementation and analysis of clinical and trans
lational studies. Our initial goal for these working groups was to leverage shared knowledge and individual experiences to facilitate more
successful collaborative and consulting relationships.
During the focus group meeting, participants first filled out an individual worksheet in which they were asked to reflect upon the qualities
of their three favourite all-time collaborators, and their three most difficult collaborators, without providing names or other identifying details.
After filling these out, Dr. Peterson and Dr. Ambrosius moderated and led the groups in completing a group worksheet listing participants'
reported positive, negative and mixed qualities. Specifically, we pushed participants to categorize qualities as “universally good,” “universally
frustrating” and “sometimes good, sometimes frustrating.” For each characteristic listed, we also asked whether participants had encountered
early signals that portended success or frustration later in the relationship. We identified 20 positive qualities that tend to yield fruitful rela
tionships with domain experts and 16 negative ones that tend to yield nothing (or worse). We also identified 15 “early signals” that might
help determine early on whether a collaboration would be positive or not. These qualities and early signals were listed in the survey, which is
described next.
2.2 | Survey formation
Following these conversations, we recognized the value of a diverse set of perspectives and decided to take the additional step of developing
a survey which targeted collaborative statisticians, allowing them to weigh in on the extent to which they agreed with the list of identified
positive and negative qualities in their interdisciplinary research teams. The characteristics and early signals identified by the working groups
were used to design the content of the survey. After consenting to the survey and providing very limited demographic data, respondents
scored their perceived importance of 20 positive qualities of domain experts (i.e., “nonstatistician” collaborators) by marking each one as one
of the following: “not needed,” “useful to have, but not critical,” “very important to have” and “absolutely must have.” The 16 negative quali
ties were scored similarly with “not problematic,” “consider avoiding domain expert,” “avoid domain expert if possible” and “avoid domain
expert at all costs.” The 15 early signals were scored as “definitely a bad sign,” “possibly a bad sign,” “no big deal, indeterminate,” “possibly a
good sign” and “definitely a good sign.” Respondents were also asked to provide any additional good, frustrating or early signals that were
not listed as text responses.
