2.3 | Survey dissemination
The survey was designed and shared using Microsoft Forms and was approved by the Colorado Multiple Institution Review Board
(22-1870). It was distributed through several channels including (1) discussion boards for American Statistical Association sections, (2) the
Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Research Design Special Interest Group, (3) all ACTStat registrants and (4) and emails to leaders of collabora
tive biostatistics groups. The only inclusion criterion was that respondents had to self-identify as a consulting or collaborating statistician. In
a message advertising the survey, we asked all readers to distribute the survey to all consulting and collaborative statisticians who might
have the experience and interest to participate. The survey was opened in May 2023, and responses were collected for this analysis through
1 July 2023.
We did not offer any incentive for participation other than to learn about the topics discussed in the focus groups; therefore, we considered
respondents volunteers and tried to minimize the survey length to respect their time and minimize dropout. We also considered the material to
be potentially sensitive. Therefore, we wanted to respect the confidentiality of participants while encouraging them to be open and transparent
about their own opinions and experiences, without fear of any grievances possibly being made public. To these ends, we did not collect demo
graphic data except for those relevant to our research questions. An archived version of our survey is available online (https://forms.office.com/r/
vZQ4uhkywn).
2.4 | Statistical analysis
We present descriptive statistics of Likert data for perceived importance of various characteristics, as well as consensus (spread) in perceived
importance among respondents. We also compute the mean Likert score (average importance) and the standard deviation (SD; consensus) to rank
qualities in terms of their importance and consensus (means and SDs and their respective confidence intervals are presented in Supporting
Information S1). We highlight notable qualities in our main manuscript as the top five most important traits, bottom five (least important) traits,
top five most consensus traits and bottom five least consensus) traits.
We used exploratory ordinal logistic regression models to quantify how scores on each quality vary by respondents' experience and position.
Models used all available data when applicable and included covariates for experience level (measured in decades) and position type (academic
faculty [reference], academic nonfaculty and non-academic). These models assume proportional odds for higher values of the outcome in the
covariates. For importance scores, odds ratios for work setting can be interpreted as the factor change in reporting higher perceived importance
of the characteristic relative to academic faculty and controlling for experience level. For simplicity, we interpret these heuristically as differences
in the general valuation of each characteristic across groups. Responses for early signals were also modelled with proportional-odds ordinal
models, and odds ratios are interpreted as the relative perceived positivity of each early signal. No adjustment was performed for multiple com
parisons; all comparisons investigated are included in the main results or supplemental and should be considered generative rather than confirma
tory of hypotheses. We chose to not include an experience by position interaction to improve the interpretability of our models; we had no prior
hypothesis that experience and position would interact. R 4.3.2 (R Core Team, 2023) was used for analyses, and the rms package version 6.7.1
(Harrell, 2001) was used for ordinal regression models.
3 | RESULTS
The survey was completed by 83 consulting or collaborative statisticians during the responding period. Available descriptive statistics of the sam
ple are described in Table 1. The sample consisted of 88% academic practicing statisticians and 12% in industry, private consulting and other set
tings. Respondents were a mix of staff (28%) and faculty (58%) respondents with a wide range of experience in statistical collaboration or
consultation (mean: 18 years, ±SD: 11 years).
Summaries of the survey responses for the most and least important positive and negative qualities are shown in Figures 1 and 2; full tabula
tions for all question responses are included in Table S1. The topmost important positive traits were that the domain expert is trustworthy, has
respect for statisticians and is capable of listening. These three traits also have high consensus, indicating they could be deal-breakers for most
statisticians if they encountered a domain expert who did not possess these qualities. The least important positive traits were “domain expert is
highly experienced in research,” “domain expert is a good leader” and “domain expert supports your career goals,” with the latter two qualities
also among those qualities with the lowest consensus.
By far, the most important negative characteristic of domain experts was the suspicion of possible research misconduct. Aside from this, most
negative qualities had low consensus among statisticians. The least important negative characteristics were “domain expert wields enough stats
knowledge to be dangerous” and “domain expert is not productive.”
