The most positively perceived early signals (Figure 3) were “the domain expert asks you what you think about a particular issue in the analysis”
and “your gut tells you the domain expert will be good to work with,” with 93% and 80%, respectively, reporting them either possibly or definitely
a good sign. The most negatively perceived early signal was “the domain expert does not include your name in an abstract despite you requesting
it,” with 81% of respondents reporting this as definitely a bad sign. Other highly negatively perceived early signals included “the domain expert
dismisses the opinions of others on the team” and “the domain expert reaches out for support shortly before a grant deadline, and the grant is
not well-written,” though for these two qualities, only about half of respondents reported this constituted a “definite” bad sign and approximately
two fifths reported it was only “possibly” a bad sign.
Associations with experience and position from ordinal regression models are shown in Figures 4 and 5, in which we highlight models where
a statistically significant ( p < .05) association was found. All associations (including qualities highlighted in Figures 1–3) are presented in
Tables S4–S6. We found that career support matters more to academic faculty than other groups, with those in academic staff or non-academic
positions considering the positive quality of “domain expert supports your career goals” more important, adjusting for experience. When framed
as the negative quality of “domain expert does not care about or support your career,” academic faculty reported similarly higher importance than
both other groups. A similar pattern emerged with big-picture thinking, which mattered significantly more to academic faculty than to other
groups.
Academic faculty generally valued the domain expert respecting the statistician's expertise and being respectful of everyone highest,
though only the comparison between academic faculty and non-academics was statistically significant. This pattern was consistent when
framed either positively or negatively. Academic faculty valued financial generosity significantly more than academic staff, though approxi
mately the same as non-academic respondents. Academic faculty valued domain expert decisiveness lower than both other groups, though
only the comparison between academic faculty and staff was statistically significant. Non-academic respondents generally attributed lower
importance to the negative quality that the “domain expert becomes unresponsive after analysis is complete, but before following through
with publication.”
When a domain expert lacks the ability to clearly articulate their research, we found that this quality is perceived more negatively by statisti
cians who have more experience and those who are academic staff. Respondents with more experience also perceived the early signal “the
domain expert reaches out for support shortly before a grant deadline, and the grant is not well-written” more negatively. Academic staff per
ceived the early signal “your gut tells you that the domain expert will be good to work with” more positively than other groups. Finally, the early
signal that “the domain expert is demanding but seems productive” was more positively perceived by nonfaculty, significantly so for non
academic respondents.
