4 | DISCUSSION
In this study, taking the perspective of collaborative statisticians, we identified the most important positive and negative qualities of collaborations
with nonstatisticians. By surveying a group of practicing statisticians, we were also able to gauge the consensus and generalizability of these quali
ties and to determine how they relate to experience level and work setting.
The near-universal consensus on the severity of suspicion of research misconduct is particularly noteworthy. While the prevalence of
research misconduct is difficult to estimate, as there is often considerable uncertainty in terms of whether a practice is misconduct or merely
“questionable,” there is no question that misconduct is a significant problem in the scientific community. One recent meta-analysis estimates that
2.9% of researchers have committed data fabrication, falsification or plagiarism and that 15.5% of researchers have witnessed others do so (Xie
et al., 2021). Ranstam et al. (2000) found in an international survey that 51% of biostatisticians knew of at least one instance of misconduct in
their proximity and 31% reported having been directly involved in a project in which misconduct took place or was about to take place. More
recently, in an audience of 90 statisticians at the American Statistical Association's Conference on Statistical Practice in 2024, 75% raised their
hands to indicate they had more probably than not witnessed academic research misconduct.1 As experts in quantitative methods embedded
broadly in collaborative research, statisticians are extremely well-positioned to detect and address research integrity issues early. We believe
implementing integrity checks to be an ethical responsibility for statisticians, but the current state of the art for such checks is in flux by necessity,
as it must continually improve to accommodate new types of data. Wang et al. (2018) performed a large US-based survey of biostatisticians to
estimate the frequency and severity of various types of inappropriate statistical analysis requests. We suggest a future direction for related work
in this area would be to gather and to document wisdom from statisticians on how they have identified and responded to different types of inap
propriate requests and how such experiences affected their statistical practice.
Aside from suspicion of misconduct, most identified negative qualities of domain experts had low consensus among statisticians. This finding
suggests that when possible, statistical groups can implement a “mix-and-match” approach for working with domain experts with perceived nega
tive qualities. One statistician's pet peeve might not matter as much to their peer. For leaders of groups with multiple statisticians, an important
takeaway is that incorporating the ability to trade or shuffle statisticians on projects may reduce friction and burnout. On the other hand, isolated
statisticians (i.e., statisticians who tend to work without other statisticians) typically do not have this option, and therefore, such positions tend to
be more difficult to sustain without careful consideration of how to handle difficult collaborations.
In addition, our study contains lessons for collaborative statisticians, who would do well to heed the negatively perceived early warning sig
nals in their collaborators, especially those that most other statisticians think should be avoided. These warning signs may not ultimately matter
for a specific collaboration but are often worth seeking mentorship about as early as possible. It is important that statisticians know how to
