the extra mile to ensure the collaboration's longevity.
Though not the intended target audience of this work, our work has implications for the training of domain expert collaborators as well. Par
ticularly, the lists of positive and negative qualities as well as the list of early signals and their perceived positivity translate to important training
considerations for domain experts who aspire to work productively with statisticians. Statisticians and statistical organizations can certainly work
to encourage the positive characteristics and discourage the negative ones, but this information might be better received when incorporated into
domain-specific researcher education.
We found differences in the valuation of certain characteristics and the positivity of early signals by work setting, and these differences
largely seem to reflect different incentives; for example, academic faculty valued support for career development most. Interestingly, more experi
enced statisticians perceived lack of clarity of research objectives and last-minute, low-quality requests significantly worse than those with less
experience. One explanation for this finding is that as their experience grows, statisticians develop a more mature understanding of the impor
tance of clear research objectives in producing impactful research. Statisticians may also be accumulating experiences of fulfilling last-minute,
low-quality requests for collaboration or consultation, which can cause a lot of stress without producing any real value. In either case, one remedy
for such situations is to formalize and enforce deadlines for requesting statistical support; even if it creates short-term frustration for domain
experts, in the long term, domain experts, statisticians and the quality of research more generally would benefit from such policies.
Our study is subject to several limitations. First, in our survey, we did not collect sufficient demographic data with which we could have better
described our study population. This is important as our survey was not administered to a random sample from a well-defined sampling frame.
This fact, alongside our not adjusting for multiplicity, renders the generalizability of these results uncertain, and all hypotheses herein should be
considered exploratory or generative. Another limitation is that domain expert characteristics change by field and institution, but we did not mea
sure this and cannot adjust for this. This work is decidedly from the statistician perspective, but a beneficial future direction would be to also sur
vey domain experts about positive and negative characteristics of collaborative statisticians. Finally, the anonymous and cross-sectional nature of
our survey precluded the possibility of estimating the survey's reliability and validity. Establishing the reliability of these questions in measuring
statistician preference and also their validity for real-world outcomes (e.g., productivity or burnout) would be welcome in future research. Despite
these limitations, our study provides a unique perspective on the importance of various qualities and early signals in the domain expert
statistician relationship.
