Ryan A. Peterson is an Assistant Professor of Biostatistics and Informatics in the Colorado School of Public Health at the University of
Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus. He works collaboratively within the Center for Innovative Design and Analysis and serves as the
Director of the Pulmonary Translational Biostatistics & Bioinformatics Core. His research interests include model selection, missing data,
statistical computation, post-selection inference, and interdisciplinary statistical collaboration.
Emily Slade is an Assistant Professor of Biostatistics at the University of Kentucky and the founder and director of the Biostatistics Consulting
and Interdisciplinary Research Collaboration Lab (Biostat CIRCL). Her research focuses on effective training and integration of biostatisticians
on collaborative research teams.
Gina-Maria Pomann is an Associate Professor of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics and the director of the Biostatistics, Epidemiology and
Research Design (BERD) Methods Core at Duke University, Durham, NC. The BERD Methods Core is a team of over 40 students, staff, and
faculty biostatisticians and data scientists in the Department of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics who collaborate with clinical and/or transla
tional researchers. QSU website: https://biostat.duke.edu/research/biostatistics-epidemiology-and-research-design-berd-methods-core.
Walter T. Ambrosius is a Professor and the Chair of the Department of Biostatistics and Data Science in the Division of Public Health
Sciences at Wake Forest University School of Medicine. His current work is primarily in coordinating centers for NIH-funded multi-center
studies, and he directs the Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design of the WFUSM Clinical and Translational Science Institute.
